//! The A64 scalar floating point and AdvSIMD translator: the FP, SIMD and crypto parts of
//! QEMU's `target/arm/tcg/translate-a64.c`.
//!
//! The `trans_` hooks are generated by [`simd_trans`] from a table that names, for every
//! pattern, the shape of the translation and the operation. The arithmetic is done by three
//! out of line helpers, `a64_neon` ([`super::super::vec_helper`]), `a64_fp`, `a64_fp_cmp` and
//! `a64_fp_gpr` ([`super::super::vfp`]) and `a64_crypto` ([`super::super::crypto`]), which take
//! the register numbers and a descriptor; moves, duplicates, immediates, FCSEL, the flag
//! setting of FCMP and the loads and stores are expanded inline as QEMU does.
//!
//! Differences from QEMU:
//!
//! - QEMU expands most integer vector operations inline with the TCG gvec ops and calls one
//!   helper per operation and element size for the rest. Here every AdvSIMD data processing
//!   instruction is one call of a helper that reads and writes the registers in `env`. The
//!   results, the FPSR flags and QC are the same; the generated code is slower.
//! - FEAT_AFP, FEAT_AH (FPCR.AH), FEAT_NEP, FEAT_RPRES and FEAT_FPRCVT are not implemented, so
//!   scalar results always clear the rest of the vector register, and the FPRCVT forms
//!   (`FCVT*` to and `SCVTF`/`UCVTF` from a general register size in a SIMD register) are
//!   unallocated, as they are in QEMU for CPUs without the feature.
//! - With SVE, QEMU clears the bits of the Z register above 128 (up to the vector length)
//!   on every AdvSIMD and FP write except the single structure loads, and so does this port
//!   (the helpers clear all 2048 bits, which is the same because the bits above the vector
//!   length are always zero, see `sve_narrow_vq()`).
//! - Data accesses are always little endian, and MTE tag checks are not done.
//! - QEMU checks the SP alignment of SIMD loads and stores through SP when the CPU asks for
//!   it; this port never does (as for the integer loads and stores).

